#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110447 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9655010-b852-53ec-a6ae-f3fc95cc50cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle in Degrees"}] :question/id #uuid "1a73bf50-94b6-536b-8213-eb3d0b3ea1a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3349" :question/problem "![](images/69/69e1bae966baea90d6f2395bd4db263d3a564f53912f67d4e0812243d28550e3.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the angle $\\theta = 100^\\circ$ in the coordinate plane. What is its reference angle $\\theta_R?$" :question/worked-solution "The reference angle, denoted $\\theta_R,$  is the angle that $\\theta=100^\\circ$ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/e4/e453e8a82b70d69009db59f161a798cb57c85602d79b4bcc44757cd2a192b860.png)\n\nSince $\\theta$ is in the second quadrant, its reference angle is given by \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & ={180}^{∘}-\\theta  \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{100}^{∘} \\\\  & ={80}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110448 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "174b9175-b7d6-5709-9db4-c3cc4f3834d0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle in Radians"}] :question/id #uuid "36c616f3-67df-5833-90a6-81ee6966e1a3" :question/math-academy-id "e-3350" :question/problem "If the angle $\\theta = \\dfrac{11\\pi}{9}$ is represented in the coordinate plane in the usual way, then what is its reference angle?" :question/worked-solution "To help us to visualize the angle, we first convert the measure of the angle into degrees. \n\nTo convert the measure of an angle in radians to an equivalent measure in degrees, we multiply the measure in radians by $\\dfrac{180^\\circ}{\\pi}.$ This gives\n\n$$\n\\left(\\dfrac{11\\pi} {9}\\right) \\cdot \\left(\\dfrac {180^\\circ} {\\pi}\\right) = \n\t220^\\circ.\n$$\n\nTherefore $\\dfrac{11\\pi} {9}$ is equivalent to $220^\\circ,$ which lies in the third quadrant.\n\n![](images/96/964e1116b6f9273321faf21f3f30c325ec81f49622eab951bc6a54823f3c7079.png)\n\nThe reference angle, denoted $\\theta_R,$  is the angle that $\\theta$ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nAs $\\theta$ is in the third quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\theta - \\pi.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\frac{11\\pi }{9}-\\pi =\\frac{2\\pi }{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110449 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8beabfbf-ac82-5bef-bec9-7f3ff980601c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "743ae44e-1a0c-5015-a292-f4e0f89cbfc1" :question/math-academy-id "e-3352" :question/problem "![](images/75/75717526542bddffb22bbf8ca2593187fea84724fdd107b1327c049e6495a57b.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle and the angle $\\theta$ is measured in the usual way. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $0.74,$ calculate the reference angle $\\theta_R$ in degrees to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "First, we create a right triangle by drawing a vertical line from $P$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/5e/5e3cf6be5c87565683466ecf91646e3587accc41cd4d1774ec6aaed007f1e809.png)\n\nSo we have the following triangle, where $\\theta_R$ is the reference angle of $\\theta.$\n\n![](images/51/51bfc8624d27c386ceaaea38ab99ac1d96ed880e5674b90c9dd00b15579b0d6a.png)\n\nWe know that the hypotenuse has a length of $1$ because it is a radius of the unit circle. \n\nNow, using the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\cos \\theta_R = \\dfrac{0.74}{1}  = 0.74.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\theta_R = \\arccos\\left(0.74\\right) \\approx 42^\\circ.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110450 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e10328e3-ff59-50d9-a3c3-ef145fe1bc16" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All of the Angles With a Given Reference Angle"}] :question/id #uuid "591bf84d-62a7-5c9b-ada2-fdb2de62462b" :question/math-academy-id "e-3353" :question/problem "An angle $\\theta$ in the coordinate plane (measured in the usual way) has a reference angle $\\theta_R = 30^\\circ.$ Which of the following cannot be $\\theta?$\n\nI. $30^\\circ$\n\nII. $150^\\circ$\n\nIII. $240^\\circ$\n\nIV. $330^\\circ$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between $\\theta_R$ and $\\theta.$\n\n![](images/0a/0af4dfb696fc96a64d470ffe4561be7d039788cb83b651e48bfd4ad2d4b86fc5.png)\n\nUsing $\\theta_R = 30^\\circ,$ let's calculate all possible values of $\\theta.$\n\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the first quadrant, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}={30}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the second quadrant, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={150}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the third quadrant, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{∘}+{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={210}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- If $\\theta$ lies in the fourth quadrant, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={360}^{∘}-{30}^{∘} \\\\  & ={330}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta$ cannot be equal to $240^\\circ.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542219 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e10328e3-ff59-50d9-a3c3-ef145fe1bc16" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All of the Angles With a Given Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8172bd17-7b08-52c6-a91f-1da350c7af41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541683} {:answer/id #uuid "a57bc90a-3599-5e7e-bf83-fcfd7c112528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541764} {:answer/id #uuid "ed47b3f3-8dc4-50a2-a254-06ff146be37f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541925} {:answer/id #uuid "f7b9d86f-cd12-5f45-8402-b77b93ef0bcd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541940} {:answer/id #uuid "ff805cc3-2110-5682-9a57-fde03f25b7f9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a57bc90a-3599-5e7e-bf83-fcfd7c112528" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\pi }{7}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541764} :answer-field/id #uuid "95bcbd8e-ecbd-5c4e-9e03-c66ca0ee2b43" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542039}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "eb0cb59e-65d1-5e3c-b429-f78e92cffa51" :question/math-academy-id "q-124355" :question/problem "An angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane (measured in the usual way) has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$ Which of the following cannot be $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between ${\\theta }_{R}$ and $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/35/35e9a04cf71b42acdf7769ef541c5b93c9267130e965d52d3989f3a00692cea1.png)\n\nUsing ${\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{7},$ let's calculate all possible values of $\\theta .$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\theta =\\pi -{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{6\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the third quadrant, then $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\pi +\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{8\\pi }{7}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the fourth quadrant, then $\\theta =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R}=2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{7}=\\frac{13\\pi }{7}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta $ cannot be equal to $\\frac{5\\pi }{7}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542112 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110689 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e10328e3-ff59-50d9-a3c3-ef145fe1bc16" :knowledge-point/title "Finding All of the Angles With a Given Reference Angle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "719747b2-4f8a-5d2f-ae3c-423b0a4ee5a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{160}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541652} {:answer/id #uuid "82fadeeb-e55c-5660-97ff-803550d79ad6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{340}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541688} {:answer/id #uuid "8df385bf-d3ec-50d3-8b4a-24044ea3c6e0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{200}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541710} {:answer/id #uuid "c8ed97ff-7d4e-5cd6-a249-ce126e68e0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{260}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541848} {:answer/id #uuid "d42b0d8f-9a44-55d5-b762-7e4b3f42753c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{20}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c8ed97ff-7d4e-5cd6-a249-ce126e68e0a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{260}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541848} :answer-field/id #uuid "9f5a90f6-3716-50e9-b5d9-508f6f90aea7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542043}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "072474b4-ff42-59ab-ad55-f18f16ce9f1c" :question/math-academy-id "q-23450" :question/problem "An angle $\\theta $ in the coordinate plane (measured in the usual way) has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={20}^{∘}.$ Which of the following cannot be $\\theta ?$" :question/worked-solution "Let's remind ourselves of the relationship between ${\\theta }_{R}$ and $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/a2/a2d02d14edf25a59eceb842a2cc902f07a29754f0978ec56a8293e16698ec383.png)\n\nUsing ${\\theta }_{R}={20}^{∘},$ let's calculate all possible values of $\\theta .$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the first quadrant, then $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}={20}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the second quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}={160}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the third quadrant, then $\\theta ={180}^{∘}+{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}+{20}^{∘}={200}^{∘}.$\n\n- If $\\theta $ lies in the fourth quadrant, then $\\theta ={360}^{∘}-{\\theta }_{R}={360}^{∘}-{20}^{∘}={340}^{∘}.$\n\nTherefore, $\\theta $ cannot be equal to ${260}^{∘}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542178 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110686 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e9655010-b852-53ec-a6ae-f3fc95cc50cb" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle in Degrees"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4bab8f0d-a06b-5e24-b3c8-3fee86207186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541577}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4bab8f0d-a06b-5e24-b3c8-3fee86207186" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541577} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b184226-aed3-5642-817b-62f7c355b527" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541984}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9f1700e6-8df7-5af8-96a1-8f4f67997ae9" :question/math-academy-id "q-246855" :question/problem "![](images/1d/1d6ba20a6e68add48e3d979971c819a8d45a5ec8473a6781a6f88797af442328.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the angle $\\theta ={200}^{∘}$ in the coordinate plane.  What is its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}?$\n\n${\\theta }_{R}=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "![](images/7e/7e7ddf9e0a6edbfd5521ca5de1d718ab9c66b7e0a96ed8d52798fd16daf63aa5.png)\n\nThe reference angle, denoted ${\\theta }_{R},$  is the angle that $\\theta ={200}^{∘}$ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nAs $\\theta $ is in the third quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\theta -{180}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}={200}^{∘}-{180}^{∘}={20}^{∘}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542208 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "174b9175-b7d6-5709-9db4-c3cc4f3834d0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298a2392-5f6c-5963-9d4b-6cfae6a70e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541506}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "298a2392-5f6c-5963-9d4b-6cfae6a70e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541506} :answer-field/id #uuid "d693fce3-a431-51fe-b507-4e6a4e61687f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542067}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c9ee09ae-09ff-5c56-b0b2-c24e54ab3c69" :question/math-academy-id "q-266024" :question/problem "![](images/44/44097b3c27d493dfb40698cdb743178a6720eb55c8019f63c4189108eb93dcf7.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the angle $\\theta =\\frac{10\\pi }{9}$ in the coordinate plane. Expressed in radians, what is its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}?$\n\n${\\theta }_{R}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/f2/f230c63f4a7768ba3e717e4ca69cea7d999fef83ae5fbaf1ebcd7af6697c1acb.png)\n\nThe reference angle, denoted ${\\theta }_{R},$  is the angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nAs $\\theta $ is in the third quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\theta -\\pi .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{10\\pi }{9}-\\pi =\\frac{\\pi }{9}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542203 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110687 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "174b9175-b7d6-5709-9db4-c3cc4f3834d0" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle in Radians"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "72a69325-85dc-5e88-8b1e-2b64e07b0f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541657}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "72a69325-85dc-5e88-8b1e-2b64e07b0f99" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\pi}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541657} :answer-field/id #uuid "9654618b-de88-5b2c-9a5f-2409f40166c8" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542040}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c65c1e13-f381-5212-802a-8ad773b76030" :question/math-academy-id "q-266026" :question/problem "![](images/6d/6d1f3a4ec50d76a52dc6a3a989d3779b529058d0de9c31d627d76a139884ff58.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows the angle $\\theta =\\frac{11\\pi }{6}$ in the coordinate plane. Expressed in radians, what is its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}?$\n\n${\\theta }_{R}\\,=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "![](images/46/4672b0e5bf7db7d8eecc659ecd988c94d7e290c426a673d82d9b3299f02dc20b.png)\n\nThe reference angle, denoted ${\\theta }_{R},$  is the angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nAs $\\theta $ is in the fourth quadrant, its reference angle is given by\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=2\\pi -\\theta .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=2\\pi -\\frac{11\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542123 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8beabfbf-ac82-5bef-bec9-7f3ff980601c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ab2cdde-dfb9-532c-8c73-75dd0056e6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8ab2cdde-dfb9-532c-8c73-75dd0056e6f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541702} :answer-field/id #uuid "079165ca-e375-5f84-a4c1-65d6ae82bdf7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541967}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1ad7aa31-ffdf-5a51-ba9d-eeaea5553edb" :question/math-academy-id "q-266057" :question/problem "![](images/59/591d9b81e6480a20388f873a6b4aad0d6712edec54f1b150f0c530e023157d48.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle and the angle $\\theta $ measured in the usual way. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $0.86,$ calculate the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ to the nearest integer.\n\n${\\theta }_{R}≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The reference angle is the acute angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we create a right-triangle by drawing a vertical line from $P$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/1b/1b11bdb22a07da238cee479070ace299f89f186e1500ec44d9af84a7f37d12f5.png)\n\nSo, we have the following triangle, where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle of $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/25/256deadcd68077b1cace4ec55d897c393b2ac849af0ba29748840a617c81e989.png)\n\nWe know that the hypotenuse has a length of $1$ because it is a radius of the unit circle. \n\nNow, using the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{0.86}{1}=0.86.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ using the inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.86)≈{31}^{∘}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542187 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8beabfbf-ac82-5bef-bec9-7f3ff980601c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fdf5e62d-fa52-524d-8093-61aeaf7595ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fdf5e62d-fa52-524d-8093-61aeaf7595ce" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541955} :answer-field/id #uuid "af13f5ae-6f73-586a-83dc-28d314005210" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542048}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ad8c8e6a-2931-50af-b1e7-7872437ed775" :question/math-academy-id "q-266076" :question/problem "![](images/64/6427becc41fd736c4161fd41fce92e76352315fd535132c1c8804b5d0949587e.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a unit circle and the angle $\\theta $ measured in the usual way. Given that the $x$-coordinate of the point $P$ is $-0.26,$ calculate the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ to the nearest integer.\n\n${\\theta }_{R}≈$\n{{field-1}}${}^{∘}$" :question/worked-solution "The reference angle is the acute angle that $\\theta $ makes with the $x$-axis.\n\nFirst, we create a right-triangle by drawing a vertical line from $P$ to the $x$-axis.\n\n![](images/40/403edda1121bb4a647a52fcff755659ab1f9fe2ea310572b13be423cef4d6db9.png)\n\nSo, we have the following triangle, where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle of $\\theta .$\n\n![](images/38/38a60a712b18a4b9575ed812f8bd184a56ae5969fe865b9edc12f2b0faccd48f.png)\n\nWe know that the hypotenuse has a length of $1$ because it is a radius of the unit circle. \n\nNow, using the triangle above, we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{0.26}{1}=0.26.\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the angle ${\\theta }_{R}$ using the inverse cosine:\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}=\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(0.26)≈{75}^{∘}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111096 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110688 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8beabfbf-ac82-5bef-bec9-7f3ff980601c" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Reference Angle Given the Coordinates of a Point on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "453de9bb-219f-51a2-a06c-2d96190a0e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541569}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "453de9bb-219f-51a2-a06c-2d96190a0e64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "66" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541569} :answer-field/id #uuid "414a4b1b-25c9-54a2-b401-cd7e99615252" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186108325}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/